use std
into clips.amni

Imports: gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.motion, cv.rig, cv.kin and spring (the local module spring.amni; its `mquat(m gfx::mat4) linalg::Quat`, the rotation of a matrix with scale removed, and `pos(m) float3`, its translation, are used unqualified). This file is a module with no main (namespace clips). It replaces the page's three.js clip baking: it loads the humanoid animation library, samples every clip at 30 frames per second on the library's own skeleton, and retargets it onto an avatar with `kin::retarget_clip`, producing the frames of 91 floats the mixer takes (22 slot local rotations x, y, z, w in floats 0..87, then the hips' local position in floats 88..90). Every module-level name starts with `kc_` because module names are used unqualified next to other modules. The rig module works in `real` (f64): convert with `as real` and `as f32` at the boundary.

Module state: `var kc_lib = -1` (the library asset), `var kc_names byte[>..]` (all baked clip names, each followed by a newline byte 10), `var kc_nf int[>..]`, `var kc_loop bool[>..]`, `var kc_frames f32[>..]` (every baked clip's frames, clip after clip), `var kc_first int[>..]` (each clip's first float in `kc_frames`), `var kc_slot int[22]` (the avatar's humanoid slot nodes, global, -1 when missing; written with a loop).

`kc_bones(asset int, out int[>..]&)` lists the asset's skeleton as global node indices: the joints (`gltf::ints[skin.joints.first + j]`, already global) of the skin in the asset's skin span (`gltf::assets[asset].skins`) with the most joints; when the asset has no skin, every node of the asset in order.

`kc_rest(asset int, root gfx::mat4, bones int[:], slot i32[:], rest real[:]) bool` maps the skeleton to the 22 humanoid slots and packs its rest. Call `motion::rest(asset)` and `motion::update(asset, root)`. Build the names text (each bone's node name `gltf::text_of(gltf::nodes[node].name)`, joined with newline bytes 10), the parent list (the index in `bones` of the node's parent, or -1 when the parent is not one of the bones) and the rest world positions (`pos(motion::world[node])`, 3 reals per bone). Call `rig::rig_map(names, bones.len, parents, positions, slot)` and return false unless `rig::rig_humanoid(slot)`. Then for each slot k with `slot[k] >= 0` (an index into bones), with node `b = bones[slot[k]]` and its parent node p: rest[12k..12k+4] is the world rotation of b (`mquat(motion::world[b])`), rest[12k+4..12k+7] its world position, rest[12k+7..12k+11] the world rotation of p (identity when b has no parent), and rest[12k+11] = 1. Unfilled slots are all zeros except an identity quaternion at 12k and 12k+7.

`kc_facing(asset int, bones int[:], slot i32[:]) gfx::mat4` is the root that makes the model face +z the way the rig expects: `gfx::mat4_identity`, except when slots 7 and 11 (left and right upper arm) are both filled and the x of slot 7's rest world position (after `motion::rest` and `motion::update(asset, gfx::mat4_identity)`) is below slot 11's, return `linalg::qmatrix(linalg::qaxis(float3 { 0.0, 1.0, 0.0 }, 3.14159265))`.

`clips_lib(path const byte[:]) bool` loads the library into `kc_lib` when it is not loaded yet (`gltf::load(path)`, not added to the scene) and returns whether it is loaded. Load it before the avatar: modules that grow the avatar later (cloth appends joints to the end of `gltf::nodes` and widens the avatar's node span) need the avatar to be the last asset loaded.

`clips_load(path const byte[:], asset int) int` bakes the library at path for the avatar asset and returns the number of clips (negative on failure: -1 library failed to load, -2 library not humanoid, -3 avatar not humanoid). Load the library with `clips_lib(path)` (return -1 when it fails). Clear `kc_names`, `kc_nf`, `kc_loop`, `kc_frames`, `kc_first`. Library: bones, `lroot = kc_facing`, then `kc_rest` into a library slot map `ls i32[22]` and rest pack `lr real[264]`. Avatar: bones, `aroot = kc_facing`, `kc_rest` into `as i32[22]` and `ar real[264]`; set `kc_slot[k]` to the avatar's bone node for each filled slot, else -1; also keep each avatar slot bone's rest local rotation (`gltf::nodes[node].r`). For every animation of the library (the asset's animation span) whose name does not end in "_RM": `nf = clamp(round(duration * 30) + 1, 2, 1000)`; for each frame i, with `t = min(duration, i / 30)`: `motion::rest(kc_lib)`, `motion::apply(anim, t, 1.0)`, `motion::update(kc_lib, lroot)`, and write 91 reals into a source frame buffer: for each slot k, the world rotation of the library slot bone (identity when unfilled) at 4k, and at 88 the world position of library slot 0's bone. Then `kin::retarget_clip(ls, lr, as, ar, nf, src, out)`. Convert to mixer frames: for each frame and slot k, floats 4k..4k+4 are the retargeted local rotation when the slot is filled in both rigs, the avatar bone's rest local rotation when filled only in the avatar, else the identity; floats 88..90 are the retargeted hips world position brought into the hips' parent space (`linalg::point(linalg::inverse(motion::world[parent of avatar slot 0 bone] after motion::rest(asset) and motion::update(asset, aroot)), p)`; the position as is when the hips have no parent). Append the frames to `kc_frames` (recording `kc_first`), nf to `kc_nf`, the clip loops when its name ends in "_Loop" or equals "A_TPose", and append the name and a newline to `kc_names`. Afterwards restore the avatar with `motion::rest(asset)` and `motion::update(asset, gfx::mat4_identity)`. Return the clip count.

`clips_count() int` is `kc_nf.len`. `clips_frames(c int) f32[:]` returns the slice of `kc_frames` holding clip c (`kc_first[c]` for `kc_nf[c] * 91` floats). `clips_name(c int, out byte[>..]&)` copies clip c's name (the c-th line of `kc_names`) into out (cleared first).
